.TH VTIUM 8 2026-11-22 "vtium 0.1.0-alpha.11" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
vtium \- a terminal for the Linux virtual console
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBvtium\fR [\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fB\-\-login\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fICOMMAND\fR...]
.br
\fBvtium\fR \fBcheck\-config\fR [\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIFILE\fR]
.br
\fBvtium\fR \fBconfig\fR [\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIARG\fR]...
.br
\fBvtium\fR \fBblank\fR \fB\-\-vt\fR \fIN\fR
.br
\fBvtium\fR \fBwake\fR \fB\-\-vt\fR \fIN\fR
.br
\fBvtium\fR \fBconsole\fR \fBenable\fR \fIN\fR|\fB\-\-all\fR
.br
\fBvtium\fR \fBconsole\fR \fBdisable\fR \fIN\fR|\fB\-\-all\fR
.br
\fBvtium\fR \fBconsole\fR \fBstatus\fR
.br
\fBvtium\fR \fBconsole\fR \fBfallback\fR \fIN\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
vtium is a terminal that runs on a Linux virtual terminal (VT), one of the text screens you switch to with Ctrl+Alt and a function key. vtium takes the place of getty, the program that normally shows the login prompt there. vtium renders each frame itself through KMS/DRM, the kernel's graphics interface, so it shows modern fonts, color emoji, 24-bit color, and images.
.PP
systemd starts vtium on a VT through one of its units, and vtium then runs a login prompt or another command in each console. \fBvtium console enable\fR chooses the VTs that vtium runs on. See UNITS below.
.PP
vtium reads its settings from \fB/etc/vtium/vtium.conf\fR when it starts, and its command line wins over the file. vtium.conf(5) describes every setting. \fBvtium check\-config\fR checks the file, and vtium-config(1) edits it.
.SH OPTIONS
These options exist only on the command line.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-configdir=VALUE\fR
The directory that holds \fBvtium.conf\fR, vtium's configuration file.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print the options and exit.
.TP
\fI\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version, the source revision of the build, and the optional features it was built with, then exit.
.PP
Every top-level setting of vtium.conf is also an option: \fB\-\-key=VALUE\fR or \fB\-\-key VALUE\fR, and \fB\-\-key\fR or \fB\-\-no\-key\fR for a switch. \fB\-\-login \-\- COMMAND...\fR sets the command that each console runs. vtium.conf(5) lists the settings.
.SH COMMANDS
A command word as the first argument runs one of these commands instead of the console.
.SS "vtium check\-config"
\fBvtium\fR \fBcheck\-config\fR [\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIFILE\fR]
.PP
[\-\-with\-frit FILE]... [\-\-without\-frit NAME]... [FILE]
.PP
Checks vtium's configuration without applying it. It reads /etc/vtium/vtium.conf (or the vtium.conf in \-\-configdir, or FILE, to check a file before you install it) and the other options, as vtium would at start.
.PP
It checks the whole file: the settings at the top, and the [look], [head] and [rules] sections. It prints each error with what vtium would skip because of it, and each warning, such as a rule that never matches, a head with too many consoles, or a look whose font set is missing.
.PP
It also checks that at least one display that is connected reaches a head that a keyboard reaches, and says what is missing if not. With no display or keyboard connected, or with \-\-no\-devices, it checks the rules alone and says so.
.PP
\-\-with\-frit FILE counts the font set file FILE, named NAME.vtfrit, as the installed font set NAME, and \-\-without\-frit NAME counts the font set NAME as not installed. vtium-config passes them for the font sets that a draft adds, replaces and removes, so that a look naming a font set the draft adds gets no warning.
.PP
It exits 0 when it finds no error or warning. It exits 6 when the only warnings are fonts that a font set needs and this machine lacks: a font that is not installed, or one that lacks a character the set checks for. vtium-config installs such a file anyway, because vtium draws the set without those fonts. Any other error or warning makes it exit 1. Notes, printed on standard output, do not count: for example a weak match, a rule that needs udev, a head no rule uses, a device no rule places, a head whose VTs have no vtium enabled, a font helper vtium would refuse, or a look whose font set has no glyph for letters of the system's language, which vtium then draws squeezed. The notes end with what vtium would do with the file at start, and the file's policy.
.PP
It only reads: vtium.conf, the font set font-name names, the font helper's owner and mode, the devices from /sys and /run/udev/data, and systemd's unit links under /etc/systemd. It starts the font helper to ask which of the language's letters a look's font set covers, and the font helper reads the installed fonts. It opens nothing under /dev and changes nothing.
.SS "vtium config"
\fBvtium\fR \fBconfig\fR [\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIARG\fR]...
.PP
vtium config runs vtium-config, the font and settings tool, with the words that follow, as the user who runs it. vtium finds vtium-config at the path that the font-helper setting names, else at the path vtium was built with.
.PP
vtium refuses to run a file that is not a regular file, that is setuid or setgid, that an account other than root or the user owns, or that its group or others can write.
.SS "vtium blank"
\fBvtium\fR \fBblank\fR \fB\-\-vt\fR \fIN\fR
.PP
Turns off the displays of the vtium on VT N now (it sends SIGRTMIN+1). Run it as root or as the user vtium runs as.
.SS "vtium wake"
\fBvtium\fR \fBwake\fR \fB\-\-vt\fR \fIN\fR
.PP
Turns on the displays of the vtium on VT N (it sends SIGRTMIN+2). Run it as root or as the user vtium runs as.
.SS "vtium console"
\fBvtium\fR \fBconsole\fR \fBenable\fR \fIN\fR|\fB\-\-all\fR
.br
\fBvtium\fR \fBconsole\fR \fBdisable\fR \fIN\fR|\fB\-\-all\fR
.br
\fBvtium\fR \fBconsole\fR \fBstatus\fR
.br
\fBvtium\fR \fBconsole\fR \fBfallback\fR \fIN\fR
.PP
enable makes vtium run on VT N, or on every VT, from its next start, and disable undoes that. N is 1 to 63, or ttyN.
.PP
\-\-direct runs vtium in direct mode, which opens the screen and the keyboards itself as root, or takes them from seatd where seatd runs and vtium is built with libseat. \-\-seat runs it in seat mode, which takes them from logind.
.PP
enable with no mode changes nothing: it says which mode fits this machine and the command to run.
.PP
enable and disable need root, and they start or stop nothing now. disable turns off either mode. status shows what runs on each VT.
.PP
fallback N is what vtium-getty@.service runs when vtium on VT N fails: it starts a login prompt (getty) on VT N, unless that would stop another program.
.SH UNITS
.TP
\fBvtium@.service\fR
Runs vtium on a VT in direct mode, as \fBvtium@ttyN.service\fR. vtium opens the displays and the keyboards itself, as root. The unit is also \fBautovt@.service\fR, the unit that logind starts on a VT you switch to. The unit conflicts with \fBgetty@.service\fR on the same VT.
.TP
\fBvtium\-seat@.service\fR
Runs vtium on a VT in seat mode, where vtium takes the displays and the keyboards from logind through libseat. A build with libseat and logind ships this unit.
.TP
\fBvtium\-getty@.service\fR
Runs \fBvtium console fallback\fR when vtium fails on its VT. That starts a login prompt on the VT, unless the closed policy forbids it.
.SH KERNEL COMMAND LINE
.TP
\fBvtium.enable=0\fR
Turns vtium off for one boot. vtium exits with status 1 before it reads anything else. Under the open policy, the getty fallback then starts a login prompt on the VT. Under the closed policy, no login prompt starts, because a login prompt would accept any keyboard.
.TP
\fBsystemd.unit=rescue.target\fR
Boots into rescue mode. vtium's units conflict with the rescue and emergency services, so vtium steps aside for them.
.TP
\fBquiet\fR, \fBloglevel=\fR
Set the kernel's console log level. vtium shows a kernel message on screen only when that level lets the message through.
.SH HELPER PROGRAMS
vtium starts two helper programs, each in a process of its own, so that it never parses a font file or an image itself.
.TP
\fBvtium\-config serve\fR
The font helper, which reads the fonts that a font set names and renders their glyphs for the console. vtium starts it at the path that the \fBfont\-helper\fR setting names.
.TP
\fBvtium\-image\fR
The image helper, in a build with image support. vtium-image decodes the images that programs send, in a sandbox, under an account of its own that its sysusers file creates. vtium starts it with the \fBimage\-helper\fR setting's path. Nobody runs it by hand.
.PP
Two programs run inside a vtium console. vtium-clipboard(1), also called \fBvticlip\fR, copies text to the console's clipboard. vtium-gallery(1), in a build with image support, shows pictures as thumbnails.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
\fBSIGTERM\fR, \fBSIGINT\fR
End vtium, with status 0.
.TP
\fBSIGRTMIN+1\fR
Turns off vtium's displays now, as \fBvtium blank\fR does.
.TP
\fBSIGRTMIN+2\fR
Turns vtium's displays on again, as \fBvtium wake\fR does.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
vtium ended on \fBSIGTERM\fR or \fBSIGINT\fR, or a console's command exited while the \fBoneshot\fR setting is on. \fB\-\-help\fR and \fB\-\-version\fR exit 0 too.
.TP
1
vtium stopped on an error. Its command line was wrong, or \fBvtium.enable=0\fR turned it off. A console's command could not start, or exited too quickly too many times. The VT hung up, or no display appeared within the time that the \fBdisplay\-wait\fR setting gives.
.PP
\fBvtium check\-config\fR exits 0, 1 or 6, as its section above says.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB/etc/vtium/vtium.conf\fR
The settings and the sections. See vtium.conf(5).
.TP
\fB/etc/vtium/fonts\fR
The font sets, each in a file \fBNAME.vtfrit\fR. Your own sets go in this folder itself. The sets vtium installs are in a folder for each category: \fBconsole\fR, \fBclassic\fR, \fBcode\fR, \fBnerd\fR, \fBscripts\fR and \fBsmall\fR. vtium looks for a set's name in this folder first, then in each category's folder.
.TP
\fB/etc/vtium/fonts/layers\fR
The layers that font sets add, each in a file \fBNAME.vtlayer\fR, such as the emoji or Nerd Fonts' icons.
.TP
\fB/usr/share/vtium/fonts\fR
The font files every vtium installs: bitmap Terminus at nine sizes, GNU Unifont, and Cullet Mono, an outline Terminus with Nerd Fonts' icons. The font sets \fBterminus\fR and \fBcullet\-mono\fR name them.
.TP
\fB/run/vtium\fR
The marker files that tell the getty fallback that a VT runs under the closed policy.
.TP
\fB/usr/share/terminfo/x/xterm\-vtium\fR
The terminfo entry that vtium sets as \fBTERM\fR in each console.
.SH SEE ALSO
vtium.conf(5), vtium-config(1), vtium-clipboard(1), vtium-gallery(1), systemd.service(5)
